10.rationale:
EXCL lens, REJECT on two independent grounds.

1) The matched hunk is incidental. `git show --stat` shows `src/inspect_ai/scorer/_math.py | 2010 +++---` — the file is wholesale rewritten. `git show 34beafda81d3~1:src/inspect_ai/scorer/_math.py | grep -n "return True"` yields exactly one hit, line 862, inside `_approx_equal`:

    def _approx_equal(ans1: Any, ans2: Any) -> bool:
        err = abs(N(ans1 - ans2))
        if err >= 1e-10:
            return False
        denom = max(abs(N(ans1)), abs(N(ans2)))
        if denom < 1e-10:
            return True
        return bool(err / denom < 1e-10)

That `return True` is not an unmeasured path: `N(ans1 - ans2)` was already evaluated and found < 1e-10, and both magnitudes are ~0, so it is the correct answer to a comparison that actually ran (absolute tolerance standing in for a meaningless relative tolerance at zero). R1 fails outright. The paired added line `expression: Any | None` is a field on the new `_ParsedValue` dataclass — a declaration, i.e. retyping/new-structure, on the exclusion list. The two lines have nothing to do with each other.

2) The commit's real subject is a security fix (`math()` fed normalized model output to SymPy `parse_expr()`, so an answer string could execute code on the evaluator host). Arbitrary code execution is not a silent pass — the measurement happened, it just also ran attacker code. And where the commit does change scoring status, it moves in the anti-silent-pass direction the rule excludes: pre-fix `score()` did `result = extract_answer(...)`, `target_expr = parse_answer(target.text)`, and `check_answers` returns `False` when either is `None`, so a failed parse (answer OR target) scored `INCORRECT`. The commit message states the new behavior — "keep model rejection incorrect while invalid targets are unscored", "Constant tuple or list answers can also enter scalar numeric comparison and raise an exception, allowing model output to fail the sample". Pre-fix therefore failed closed against the model; R2 explicitly disqualifies values in the alarming direction. The remaining changes in the diff are an arithmetic/tolerance correction ("exact integers/rationals no longer fall through to floating tolerance"), a concurrency change (`anyio.to_process` → `anyio.to_thread`), docs/CHANGELOG, lockfile/requirements, and 965 lines of new tests — all exclusion-list categories.

No pre-fix path was found where a skipped or failed measurement yielded CORRECT. Uncertainty resolves to reject; here there is little uncertainty.
